# Caching behind an interface

When a lookup is slow, wrap it rather than editing it. A caching wrapper has the same methods as the real implementation, holds it, and answers repeated requests from memory. Callers cannot tell the difference, and the slow code stays simple.

```python
class SlowPrices:
    def price(self, sku):
        print("asking the slow service")
        return 3

class CachedPrices:
    def __init__(self, inner):
        self.inner = inner
        self.cache = {}

    def price(self, sku):
        if sku not in self.cache:
            p = self.inner.price(sku)
            self.cache[sku] = p
        return self.cache[sku]

cached = CachedPrices(SlowPrices())
print(cached.price("A"), cached.price("A"))
```

## Expiry needs a clock

Cached data goes stale, so give each entry an age limit. Do not read the system clock inside the cache: take a clock as a parameter, a function returning seconds. The application passes `time.monotonic`; a test passes a fake it can move forward, and expiry becomes a plain assertion instead of a wait.

